// expect-ok
// Z3 corpus #10: axis-1 — multi-party 3-of-3 approval (distinct types).
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE PROVES
//
// A privileged `Action` actor REQUIRES three distinct caps to spawn:
// `AliceAuth`, `BobAuth`, `CarolAuth`. The type system enforces:
//   1. All three caps must be present at spawn time (arity check on init).
//   2. Each cap's TYPE is distinct, so Alice's approval cannot be
//      substituted for Bob's (or vice versa) — even if a malicious
//      actor possessed two copies of one party's cap, it could not
//      satisfy Action's init signature.
//   3. Each cap is linear → cannot be reused after spawn (O001).
//   4. Each cap must originate from a legitimate source (capability.rs
//      structural check + Z3 forge detection).
//
// The encoding generalizes to N-of-N for any compile-time-fixed N: add
// more init params with more cap types.
//
// RUNTIME SEMANTICS (VERIFIED)
//
// `spawn::<Action>(alice, bob, carol)` at the runtime layer maps to:
//   * `caps = [alice, bob]` — user-supplied init caps (stored as
//     Action's state).
//   * `fuel_cap = carol` — the runtime's per-actor fuel budget; the
//     spawn convention treats the LAST arg as fuel_cap and earlier args
//     as user caps. This means Action's third Approval (`carol`) serves
//     dual purpose: it's required for type-level authorization AND it
//     funds Action's runtime execution budget.
//
// For policies that need a SEPARATE fuel cap distinct from approvals,
// add an explicit fuel-typed param: `init(a: AliceAuth, b: BobAuth,
// c: CarolAuth, fuel: Fuel)` and pass four args at spawn.
//
// Verified end-to-end: `sigil run` executes this fixture, spawns Action,
// audit log records the spawn. See `multi_party_approval_runs_end_to_end`
// in tests/z3_corpus.rs.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT SOLVE (deferred axis-1 work, precisely scoped)
//
// * Per-actor party isolation and M-of-N quorum share one root
//   blocker: cap accumulation in coordinator state. The pattern
//   "receive Alice's cap, store it; receive Bob's cap, store it;
//   when count >= M, act" requires reassigning a non-primitive
//   state field (e.g. Option<Cap> or an enum holder). The Phase-1
//   type-checker explicitly rejects this with the diagnostic
//   "cannot reassign variable of type `X` — only primitive types
//   support reassignment in Phase 1". Fixture
//   `12_quorum_accumulation_wall.sigil` locks the diagnostic; when
//   the language relaxes the restriction (Phase 2 type system),
//   that fixture's expect-error flips and inter-actor 3-of-3 plus
//   M-of-N become writable in the same iteration.
//
// * Time-bound approvals: this gap is CLOSED in fixture
//   `11_time_bound_approval.sigil` using only existing primitives
//   (FFI `time_now()` + runtime deadline check). The cap itself
//   doesn't carry a deadline in its type — a stronger form
//   (deadline-typed caps where use-after-deadline is statically
//   impossible) would still be a real new primitive, deferred and
//   regression-locked in fixture `14_deadline_typed_cap_wall.sigil`
//   (step 33).
module sigil;

cap type AliceAuth {}
cap type BobAuth {}
cap type CarolAuth {}

actor Action {
    init(a: AliceAuth, b: BobAuth, c: CarolAuth) {}
    on Fire() -> i64 { return 1; }
}

entry actor Main {
    state {
        alice: AliceAuth,
        bob: BobAuth,
        carol: CarolAuth,
    }

    on Start() -> i64 {
        let _action = spawn::<Action>(alice, bob, carol);
        return 1;
    }
}
